WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.402
This is Unite.

2
00:00:01.402 --> 00:00:04.698
It's a place for people to work out, together,

3
00:00:04.698 --> 00:00:08.064
what a good future looks like in ordinary life.

4
00:00:08.064 --> 00:00:11.500
And then to make sure something actually happens.

5
00:00:12.302 --> 00:00:15.388
In about eleven minutes I'll follow one person

6
00:00:15.388 --> 00:00:18.605
through the whole thing: a private conversation,

7
00:00:18.605 --> 00:00:22.150
the picture of what everyone said, a live discussion,

8
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:25.762
a decision, and what the people in charge did about it.

9
00:00:26.564 --> 00:00:29.542
Most consultation stops at collecting opinions.

10
00:00:29.542 --> 00:00:32.582
Unite is built around the part that comes after.

11
00:00:32.582 --> 00:00:35.374
Nothing is asked of you here unless somebody

12
00:00:35.374 --> 00:00:38.104
has already promised, in writing, to answer.

13
00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.273
Here's an example.

14
00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:46.175
The town, the council and the people in it are invented, but the problem is real.

15
00:00:46.175 --> 00:00:49.198
As AI changes local jobs, what would let

16
00:00:49.198 --> 00:00:52.365
people here keep earning, and keep learning?

17
00:00:53.168 --> 00:00:54.505
This is the pledge.

18
00:00:54.505 --> 00:00:58.248
The council and the college have set aside a quarter of

19
00:00:58.248 --> 00:01:01.857
a million pounds and the college's evening timetable.

20
00:01:01.857 --> 00:01:04.798
They've said what's off the table, and why.

21
00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:07.738
And they've promised to answer the top five

22
00:01:07.738 --> 00:01:10.478
priorities by the eighteenth of December.

23
00:01:11.281 --> 00:01:12.637
Why insist on this?

24
00:01:12.637 --> 00:01:16.772
A study of five hundred and seventy-one proposals from local

25
00:01:16.772 --> 00:01:21.788
participation in Spain found only about a third were carried out in full.

26
00:01:21.788 --> 00:01:25.042
What made the difference was whether the people

27
00:01:25.042 --> 00:01:27.821
with power were behind it from the start.

28
00:01:28.623 --> 00:01:29.431
Meet Amina.

29
00:01:29.431 --> 00:01:31.656
She works nights in a care home.

30
00:01:31.656 --> 00:01:35.363
She starts where everyone starts: with a conversation,

31
00:01:35.363 --> 00:01:39.003
on her own, before she's seen what anyone else thinks.

32
00:01:39.804 --> 00:01:43.176
The interviewer is an AI, and it says so.

33
00:01:43.176 --> 00:01:45.842
It asks questions and it listens.

34
00:01:45.842 --> 00:01:52.114
It has no opinions, it gives no advice, and what she types stays private to her.

35
00:01:52.915 --> 00:01:56.608
It doesn't ask what she thinks about the future of work.

36
00:01:56.608 --> 00:01:58.228
It asks about yesterday.

37
00:01:58.228 --> 00:02:01.013
Real days are more reliable than opinions.

38
00:02:01.013 --> 00:02:04.835
In this recording the interviewer is using fixed questions.

39
00:02:10.211 --> 00:02:14.258
Then it asks about one moment that mattered, and why.

40
00:02:14.258 --> 00:02:15.981
One question at a time.

41
00:02:25.136 --> 00:02:29.776
Watch what happens when she says the course is too expensive.

42
00:02:29.776 --> 00:02:32.395
It doesn't file that under "cost".

43
00:02:32.395 --> 00:02:36.063
It asks what stops her, and she says it her way:

44
00:02:36.063 --> 00:02:39.805
the classes are on while she's at work or asleep.

45
00:02:39.805 --> 00:02:41.676
Her words stay her words.

46
00:02:43.625 --> 00:02:49.431
Then an ordinary weekday, a few years from now, in a life that's going well.

47
00:02:49.431 --> 00:02:50.562
Not a fantasy.

48
00:02:50.562 --> 00:02:51.315
A weekday.

49
00:02:59.276 --> 00:03:04.427
There are eight short stages like this, about twelve minutes in all.

50
00:03:04.427 --> 00:03:06.966
She can skip any of them, or stop.

51
00:03:15.687 --> 00:03:19.384
At the end the interviewer shows her what it heard.

52
00:03:19.384 --> 00:03:22.227
Five points, built from her own phrases.

53
00:03:23.028 --> 00:03:24.135
She fixes one.

54
00:03:24.135 --> 00:03:27.454
She scraps another, and adds one of her own.

55
00:03:27.454 --> 00:03:30.774
She decides how each is shown: counted only,

56
00:03:30.774 --> 00:03:33.798
shown without her name, or shown with it.

57
00:03:39.569 --> 00:03:42.435
Only what she approves leaves the conversation.

58
00:03:42.435 --> 00:03:46.912
When researchers ran AI interviews like this with several hundred people,

59
00:03:46.912 --> 00:03:50.554
ninety-nine percent approved the summary of their own views.

60
00:03:50.554 --> 00:03:52.583
Here, approval isn't a statistic.

61
00:03:52.583 --> 00:03:53.419
It's the gate.

62
00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:57.240
Now, and only now, she sees everyone else.

63
00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.540
This is the picture: what forty-nine people in

64
00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.840
her town have said, grouped by what it's about.

65
00:04:04.641 --> 00:04:07.761
Her point about classes running while she's

66
00:04:07.761 --> 00:04:11.024
asleep sits here, with eighteen other people.

67
00:04:11.024 --> 00:04:14.641
The name of the theme is a phrase one of them used.

68
00:04:15.441 --> 00:04:18.748
Every sentence in a summary can be opened.

69
00:04:18.748 --> 00:04:23.054
Here are the words behind it, and the number of people.

70
00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:27.361
The counting is done by ordinary code, never by the AI.

71
00:04:28.162 --> 00:04:32.596
If a summary gets it wrong, anyone can say so, right here.

72
00:04:32.596 --> 00:04:37.782
That report is shown beside the summary, with what was done about it.

73
00:04:38.581 --> 00:04:41.737
And the picture is honest about who's missing.

74
00:04:41.737 --> 00:04:45.766
Only three people under twenty-five have been heard so far.

75
00:04:45.766 --> 00:04:47.041
Ten were hoped for.

76
00:04:47.842 --> 00:04:52.991
From the picture, Unite drafts statements people can agree or disagree with.

77
00:04:52.991 --> 00:04:55.532
Each one lists the points it rests on.

78
00:04:56.333 --> 00:05:00.109
Amina answers first, and only then sees how others answered.

79
00:05:00.109 --> 00:05:04.752
Experiments on the wisdom of crowds show why: when people see each other's

80
00:05:04.752 --> 00:05:09.023
answers first, the answers bunch together without getting any better.

81
00:05:09.825 --> 00:05:12.040
Some statements are common ground.

82
00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:15.711
Some aren't, and Unite publishes those just as carefully.

83
00:05:15.711 --> 00:05:19.445
This one split the room: nobody should lose pay to retrain.

84
00:05:20.247 --> 00:05:24.180
Under every statement are reasons for and reasons against,

85
00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.913
side by side, in the order they arrived.

86
00:05:26.913 --> 00:05:27.580
No likes.

87
00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.047
No replies to people.

88
00:05:29.047 --> 00:05:30.247
Just the argument.

89
00:05:31.049 --> 00:05:36.505
When people really differ, a few of them are drawn by lot into a live room.

90
00:05:36.505 --> 00:05:40.669
Six people who see it differently, and forty-five minutes.

91
00:05:41.468 --> 00:05:43.390
The facilitator is an AI.

92
00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:48.444
It keeps time and turns, and it invites the people who haven't spoken.

93
00:05:48.444 --> 00:05:51.861
It never takes a side and never sums anyone up.

94
00:05:51.861 --> 00:05:55.918
In one study on Stanford's online deliberation platform,

95
00:05:55.918 --> 00:05:59.548
nearly a thousand people met in rooms run this way.

96
00:06:08.264 --> 00:06:12.429
An earlier room came back with new wording: paid learning hours,

97
00:06:12.429 --> 00:06:16.465
with the cost shared between the employer and the skills fund.

98
00:06:16.465 --> 00:06:18.644
That version became common ground.

99
00:06:20.512 --> 00:06:23.498
Not everyone can meet at a set time.

100
00:06:23.498 --> 00:06:26.968
So a room can also be slow: open for days,

101
00:06:26.968 --> 00:06:32.052
where you write where you stand before you can read the others.

102
00:06:32.852 --> 00:06:38.571
And anyone can talk a statement through in private, with the AI as a go-between.

103
00:06:38.571 --> 00:06:42.197
It shows what people who answered differently said,

104
00:06:42.197 --> 00:06:46.312
in their own words, and asks what wording you could accept.

105
00:06:47.113 --> 00:06:51.868
The rule for deciding was fixed in the pledge, before anyone spoke.

106
00:06:51.868 --> 00:06:55.435
And on every statement Unite says, in plain words,

107
00:06:55.435 --> 00:06:57.883
what's still needed for it to pass.

108
00:06:58.684 --> 00:07:01.887
The moment statements pass, everyone is asked the

109
00:07:01.887 --> 00:07:04.834
next question: which three matter most to you?

110
00:07:05.635 --> 00:07:08.919
Amina picks hers, and the list counts her in.

111
00:07:08.919 --> 00:07:13.631
It's ordered so that once a large group's first choice is placed,

112
00:07:13.631 --> 00:07:16.201
their other choices count for less.

113
00:07:16.201 --> 00:07:19.485
That way smaller groups reach the top as well.

114
00:07:20.287 --> 00:07:23.845
The top five go to the council and the college,

115
00:07:23.845 --> 00:07:28.367
who have until the eighteenth of December to answer each one.

116
00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:33.756
To see what happens next, here's a second example that's further along.

117
00:07:33.756 --> 00:07:38.789
A network of volunteer centres asked what a shared volunteering tool should do.

118
00:07:39.589 --> 00:07:41.727
Five decisions came out of it.

119
00:07:41.727 --> 00:07:45.658
The host had to answer every one with one of four words:

120
00:07:45.658 --> 00:07:48.417
accepted, changed, delayed, or refused.

121
00:07:48.417 --> 00:07:49.589
And give reasons.

122
00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:51.868
This one was refused.

123
00:07:51.868 --> 00:07:55.428
The network won't pay travel costs through the tool,

124
00:07:55.428 --> 00:07:57.711
because there's no budget for it.

125
00:07:57.711 --> 00:08:00.600
A refusal with reasons is still an answer.

126
00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:01.540
Silence isn't.

127
00:08:02.341 --> 00:08:03.986
This one was accepted.

128
00:08:03.986 --> 00:08:06.703
A volunteer proves who they are once,

129
00:08:06.703 --> 00:08:10.421
and can share that proof with any group they choose.

130
00:08:11.222 --> 00:08:14.525
Every decision carries numbered tests for "done",

131
00:08:14.525 --> 00:08:17.762
written so the people who made it can check them.

132
00:08:18.563 --> 00:08:22.574
Builders proposed how to meet those tests, and the people

133
00:08:22.574 --> 00:08:26.794
who made the decision confirmed the plan was faithful to it.

134
00:08:26.794 --> 00:08:31.013
For software, AI coding agents can do much of the building,

135
00:08:31.013 --> 00:08:34.333
with a named person accountable for every piece.

136
00:08:35.135 --> 00:08:38.421
Then it's checked, by a panel drawn by lot.

137
00:08:38.421 --> 00:08:39.690
Not by the host.

138
00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:41.781
Two tests met, and one not.

139
00:08:41.781 --> 00:08:45.515
So the record says: delivered, with small changes.

140
00:08:46.317 --> 00:08:49.915
Every decision, in every assembly, lands in one public register.

141
00:08:49.915 --> 00:08:51.576
Delivered with small changes.

142
00:08:51.576 --> 00:08:52.296
In progress.

143
00:08:52.296 --> 00:08:52.794
Delayed.

144
00:08:52.794 --> 00:08:53.237
Refused.

145
00:08:54.038 --> 00:08:56.569
"In progress" is never counted as done.

146
00:08:56.569 --> 00:09:00.620
After France's climate convention, the government counted about

147
00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:04.544
two thirds of the measures as implemented in full or in part.

148
00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:08.974
An independent count found about one in five implemented as proposed.

149
00:09:08.974 --> 00:09:14.038
So here the categories are fixed in advance, and someone else does the counting.

150
00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:19.362
And Amina has her own page: what happened to what I said.

151
00:09:19.362 --> 00:09:22.715
Each of her points, traced to the theme it

152
00:09:22.715 --> 00:09:25.990
joined and the statements that rest on it.

153
00:09:26.791 --> 00:09:29.006
Not everything starts with a host.

154
00:09:29.006 --> 00:09:30.841
Open listening is always on.

155
00:09:30.841 --> 00:09:33.626
Anyone, anywhere, can have the conversation

156
00:09:33.626 --> 00:09:36.411
about their own life, in their own language.

157
00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:39.766
What they approve joins a wider picture.

158
00:09:39.766 --> 00:09:42.260
These are people in fourteen countries.

159
00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:44.130
All invented for this example.

160
00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:50.214
Unite shows which themes keep coming up with nobody yet responsible for them.

161
00:09:50.214 --> 00:09:55.700
This one kept coming up: looking after an ageing parent while holding down a job.

162
00:09:56.503 --> 00:09:59.594
That's the opening for someone to step forward.

163
00:09:59.594 --> 00:10:05.002
Here a coalition of care workers, providers and family carers has drafted a pledge.

164
00:10:05.002 --> 00:10:07.964
It's published, and for nine more days anyone

165
00:10:07.964 --> 00:10:10.733
can challenge the question before it opens.

166
00:10:11.534 --> 00:10:14.261
Unite has to live by its own rules.

167
00:10:14.261 --> 00:10:17.973
On every page there's a button to give feedback,

168
00:10:17.973 --> 00:10:21.154
and it works the same way: a conversation.

169
00:10:21.955 --> 00:10:25.442
It never argues and never defends the product.

170
00:10:25.442 --> 00:10:30.415
What you've said so far is always on screen, and you can change it.

171
00:10:42.074 --> 00:10:45.315
Afterwards you get a private link that shows

172
00:10:45.315 --> 00:10:48.267
what happened to your feedback, and why.

173
00:10:48.267 --> 00:10:52.012
The people who run Unite are a host like any other.

174
00:10:52.012 --> 00:10:57.844
Their pledge, still a draft, says they answer by a date, and someone else checks.

175
00:10:58.646 --> 00:11:01.765
Everything you've seen is open: the rules,

176
00:11:01.765 --> 00:11:06.336
the prompts the AI is given, and the record of every assembly.

177
00:11:07.136 --> 00:11:10.596
What Unite needs now is a first real question,

178
00:11:10.596 --> 00:11:14.351
with a real host who's ready to promise an answer.

179
00:11:14.351 --> 00:11:17.516
If that could be you, start a conversation.
